#Conclusion

Having analysed e-commerce, we can clearly see that online shopping is continually evolving. Initially there were early forms of online shopping, for instance Michael Aldrich’s online shopping system in 1979. Then, in recent times, sites such as Amazon and eBay have helped increase its popularity. Online shopping is now often used by people as it is a convenient method of purchasing goods.
From a societal point of view, online shopping can be a time saving option. People can purchase goods without needing to go out of their house and can even search various sites to compare prices. Customers also benefit from the additional options available to them. Should a particular product no longer be available in store for example, people can search online and perhaps locate it elsewhere. Despite the positives however, high street stores can suffer due to people favouring online shopping. People must also be wary of entering personal and financial information onto sites.
From a business perspective, e-commerce allows for further distribution of goods. Unlike before, firms are no longer restricted to only selling in specific geographical areas. Furthermore, businesses can reduce expenditure on aspects such as rent and power alongside other store related costs. Online ordering and stock control can also often be simplified. Nonetheless, there are issues which businesses must address. Intense online competition exists and firms must ensure that their sites function effectively. Appropriate delivery arrangements also need to be in place and customer satisfaction has to be maintained.
In addition to analysing issues and duties linked to e-commerce, we also explored online security. Customers must feel confident that their private and financial data is secure. Should a business fail to adequately secure this information, then it may well suffer in terms of both customer numbers and reputation. Therefore, firms must recognise and address these potential concerns so that people can confidently engage in online shopping.
To summarise, I believe that e-commerce has provided benefits for both customers and businesses. Nevertheless, it has presented high street stores with issues and introduced novel online security concerns. Although there are positive and negative elements relating to e-commerce, online shopping has now become an integral part of daily life. I anticipate that it will continue to develop further in the future and so businesses will have to persistently strive to further enhance their online provision.